{"id":"07a67dfd-b5ed-4c47-b111-c50075af24bb","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025810,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(n.get('parent'), []).append(nid)\\n    if not node_map:\\n        raise ValueError('No nodes')\\n    # initial retained set: all node ids\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove root\\n        if n['depth'] == 0 or n.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove if symbol is required\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove if not removable\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # cannot remove if any child is still retained\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        # check all required symbols still represented after removal\\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_after = {node_map[nid2]['symbol'] for nid2 in new_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\\n            retained = new_retained\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The problem is about grammar-guided node reduction on a tree of nodes. We have nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The goal is to decide which nodes to remove (those that are removable, and not required, and whose removal doesn't cause loss of required symbols, and possibly using some ordering based on depth and tie-breaker by id). The output structural schema requires two arrays: removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Both sorted? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (probably alphabetically?), and removed_node_ids in the order they were removed (actual removal order). But the output schema doesn't specify sorting, but the description says sorted retained IDs and actual removal order. Let's interpret: retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe ascending string order), and removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the order in which we decided to remove them). The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). For nodes of same depth, use node ID as tie break (stable tie). For each node, if removable and not required, and all required symbols remain represented after its removal, then remove it. But also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must check if any children (descendants?) are retained; but if we process deeper first, children are deeper, so they will be processed before parents. So when considering a node, if any of its descendants have been retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, the set of symbols represented by retained nodes (including current node? Actually after removal, we consider the new set) must still include all required_symbols. Because we might be removing a node that has a required symbol, but that symbol might also be present elsewhere? The required_symbols list includes symbols that must appear in the tree after reduction. So we need to check that after removal, for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node (including possibly not yet considered nodes? But we process depth-first? Actually we are building the set of retained nodes incrementally. We start with all nodes as retained? And then we consider removing some. The typical approach: initially all nodes are candidates for retention (but root must always be retained). We iterate over nodes in depth order, and for each node that is removable and not required, we tentatively mark it for removal, then check if the resulting set of retained nodes (including those not yet processed? Actually we process deeper first, so children are processed before parents. When we decide to remove a child, its parent might later be removed if conditions allow. But \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained (not removed), we cannot remove the node. So children must be removed before the parent can be removed. That aligns with processing deeper first. So we can process in decreasing depth, and for each node, if it is removable and not required and none of its children are retained (i.e., all children have been removed or had no children? Actually need to check that all children are either not present or have also been removed. But we can simply check if any child's id is in retained set. Initially all nodes are in retained set? Or we start with all nodes, then we remove nodes as we go. The algorithm: Let retained = set of all node ids (or a set from all nodes). For each node in order (deepest first, tie by id), if node is removable and not required and node.id not in required? Actually required_symbols are symbols, not node ids. We need to check if node.symbol is in required_symbols? No, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must not remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? The description says \"a required symbol\". That means if the node's symbol is one of the required symbols, we cannot remove it. So we must check node.symbol in required_symbols. Also root: root.id? The root has depth 0 and parent null, we should never remove it. So we can check node.depth == 0 or node.parent is None.\n\nThen \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removing this node, among the nodes we have decided to retain (including all nodes that are not yet processed? Actually we are building the retained set. We need to check if the set of symbols from retained nodes (including nodes that will be retained after processing) still covers required_symbols. Since we process deeper first, nodes we have already processed and decided to retain (or not removed) are fixed. But there may be nodes we haven't processed yet (shallower). If we remove this node, the symbols from shallower nodes might still cover the missing symbol. So we need to consider all nodes that will be retained after the algorithm completes. That is complicated. However, typical grammar-guided reduction ensures that the reduced tree is a subtree of the original tree (only removals, no additions). So the set of retained nodes is a subset of original nodes that forms a connected subtree (actually root must be retained, and if a node is retained, its parent must be retained? Not necessarily; if we remove a parent, we might still retain children? But the rule \"node with retained children\" prevents removal of parent if any child is retained. So the retained set is a rooted tree: root retained, and for any retained node, its parent must also be retained (unless parent is null). Because if a parent is removed, its children cannot be retained (since parent removal would break tree structure? Actually the output is just set of retained node ids; the tree structure might not be enforced, but the algorithm ensures consistency through parent-child constraints). The description: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained, we cannot remove it. So the retained set is \"downward closed\": if a node is retained, all ancestors up to root must also be retained (since if an ancestor were removed, the ancestor would have a retained child (the node) and thus cannot be removed). So indeed retained set is a rooted subtree.\n\nThus, when processing in depth order (deepest first), we decide removals. At the time we consider a node, its children have already been considered (deeper). If any child is retained, then this node cannot be removed. So condition: if node.removable and node.symbol not in required_symbols and node.depth != 0 (or parent not None) and no child is retained. Then we tentatively remove it: we check if after removal, the set of symbols from retained nodes (including this node? Actually we are removing it, so its symbol is lost. But required symbols must still be present among the remaining retained nodes. At this point, the retained nodes are: all nodes we have not yet removed (including shallower ones not processed, but those will be processed later). Since we process deeper first, shallower nodes are still in retained set. So the set of symbols from retained nodes includes symbols from the current node (if we don't remove) and from all other nodes that are not yet removed (which includes all shallower nodes and any deeper nodes we retained). When we consider removal, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one other node (either already retained deeper or shallower to be retained) that has that symbol. But we don't know future decisions; we could assume that shallower nodes will be retained or not depending on their children. However, since we process deeper first, we can be certain about deeper nodes (already processed), but shallower nodes are pending. To be safe, we could check the following: after removing this node, is there at least one node that will be retained (either already retained or will be retained in the future) for each required symbol? Since we haven't yet decided on shallower nodes, we need a conservative approach. The typical algorithm for grammar-guided reduction works by ensuring that at each step, the set of retained nodes still satisfies the requirement. Since we are building the set incrementally by removing nodes, we start with all nodes retained. Then we consider removing nodes one by one, checking the condition after removal. If condition holds, we actually remove it (update retained set). If condition fails, we keep it. That approach ensures that after all removals, the final retained set satisfies the condition. And the order is as specified: deeper first, tie by id. This is simpler: start with retained = set of all node ids. Then sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending (tie break). For each node in that order:\n- if node.id not in retained? (should be, initially all)\n- if node.depth == 0: skip (root cannot be removed)\n- if node.symbol in required_symbols: skip (required symbol)\n- if node.removable is False: skip\n- Check if any child (nodes whose parent == node.id) is in retained? Because if a child is retained, we cannot remove parent. Since we process deeper first, children are deeper, so they have been processed earlier. If we had decided to remove a child, that child would not be in retained set. So we need to check if any child is still retained. If yes, skip.\n- Now, tentatively remove: create a temporary set new_retained = retained - {node.id}\n- Check if for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in new_retained (or possibly future? But we are only considering current set; future removals can only reduce the set, so if the requirement is satisfied now, it will be satisfied later because we only remove more nodes. However, if we keep this node, we might later remove other nodes that make requirement fail. But that's fine because we only check at removal time. So we must ensure that after removal, the condition holds. So we compute the set of symbols from all nodes in new_retained (including those not yet processed). Does the set contain all required_symbols? If yes, then we can remove: remove node.id from retained and add to removed list. Else, we keep it.\n\nAt the end, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)), removed_node_ids in order we removed them (the order of processing). We should output both.\n\nWe need to parse the payload. The case object has nodes list and required_symbols list. Also _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nImplement solve(case) returning dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nNow, produce the Python source code as a JSON-escaped string inside {\"implementation\": \"<code>\"}.\n\nWe must ensure no external libraries, only standard. Use json for output but solve returns dict that will be serialized by evaluator? Actually the instruction: \"The function must return a JSON-serializable object\". So we can return a Python dict. So solve returns dict.\n\nWe must handle errors: Raise ValueError for infeasible (e.g., required symbol not present at all originally). But we can assume inputs are valid.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nWe'll define solve(case):\n- Extract nodes list and required_symbols set.\n- Build a dict mapping id to node data for quick lookup.\n- Also build list of children for each node (ids) for parent-child check.\n- Initialize retained set with all node ids.\n- Initialize removed_order list.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id.\n- For each node in that order:\n    - if node.id not in retained: continue (should not happen)\n    - if node.depth == 0 or node.parent is None: skip (root)\n    - if node.symbol in required_symbols: skip\n    - if not node.removable: skip\n    - Check if any child (node id in children dict) has id in retained. If yes, skip.\n    - Compute symbols from retained set (excluding this node). For all retained ids (other nodes) get symbols.\n    - If all required symbols are in that set, then remove: retained.remove(node.id), removed_order.append(node.id)\n- Finally, sort retained list (string sort).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nWe need to handle case where removed_order is empty.\n\nEdge: If a node has multiple children, we check all.\n\nLet's code.\n\nNow produce output as JSON with implementation key. The Python source must be a single string. We'll need to escape properly. We'll write the code, then manually escape it or use a JSON serializer in our mind. Since we are writing a JSON response, we need to produce a valid JSON object. We'll write the implementation string line by line, using double quotes, escaping newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, and double quotes as \\\". We'll ensure it's valid.\n\nWe'll use triple quotes in Python source for readability, but in JSON we need to escape. We'll write a clean function.\n\nLet's do it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3239,"total_tokens":5411,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2793},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}